  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1208 PM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nCurrent satellite imagery shows increasing cloud cover over the high \nterrain, with the radar already indicating a few virga showers \ninitiating off the Front Range. Virga showers are expected through \nthe early evening for mountains and I-25 corridor before moving east \nthrough late evening. ACARS soundings today have a slight \nincrease in mid-level moisture compared to yesterday, which will \nlikely result in more shower coverage. However, due to the dry air\nmass at the lower levels, little-to-no precipitation is expected \nto reach the ground for the plains. Gusty outflow winds of 40-45 \nmph will be the main concern, however can't rule out an isolated \nstronger gust.\n\nOne thing we will keep our eye on later this afternoon and evening \nis for any storm that tracks into the far northeastern plains. \nSurface observations and CAMs this morning have consistently shown \nbetter low-level moisture and thus better instability, with modeled \nSkew-T soundings showing better shear profiles compared to farther \nwest. If a storm can tap into this environment, it could become \nstrong to severe. Hail and strong outflow winds will be the main \nthreat. \n\nWith the increase in moisture and cloud cover, we likely won't be as \nhot today as were were yesterday. However, highs should still get up \nto the upper 90s, with the potential for DEN to break its record \nhigh temperature (98 dg set in 2021). Surface observations show \ncurrent temperatures in the mid-90s, so we only need to heat up a \nfew more degrees. Barring any evaporative cooling over due to a \nvirga shower, we are on track to tie or break the record. \nOvernight low temperatures should be cooler than last night, thus \nhave no plans on extending the Heat Advisory and will let it \nexpire this evening.\n\nHeat will linger Monday and Tuesday, with the weather pattern \nlooking very similar to the past couple of days due to the upper \nlevel ridge situated in the Desert Southwest. Both days will likely \nshowers and thunderstorms in the afternoon, with best chances for \nprecipitation in the high terrain given better moisture \navailability. However, like the past couple of days, a few scattered \nvirga showers should spill onto the I-25 Corridor and east. Given \nthe overall lack of forcing, strong outflow winds will continue to \nbe the main concern. \n\nThe weather pattern begins to change on Wednesday, as the upper \nlevel ridge that has been dominating our weather for the past week\nbegins to weaken and shift east. This will result in southwest \nflow aloft for Colorado, with better monsoonal moisture being \nadvected into the region. Showers and thunderstorms will begin \nincreasing in coverage by Wednesday, however ensemble guidance \nindicates we won't get widespread beneficial rainfall till \nThursday/Friday. With above normal precipitable water values, \nlocalized heavy rainfall will likely become a concern. But, it is \ntoo soon to pinpoint exact location of biggest concern yet. WPC \nhas introduced a Slight Risk for much of the plains for Day 5 \n(Thursday) which seems reasonable at this time given how guidance \nhas been trending. Along with this precipitation, expect cooler \ntemperatures by Thursday and possibly lasting through the weekend.\nNBM has trended slightly warmer with forecasted highs now in the \nlow 80s. These still may change in the next few days, as models \nare still coming into agreement on exactly how much we will cool \noff. GEFS has been consistently warmer that the ECMWF Ens. Expect\nhighs to be somewhere between low- mid 80s.\n\nFor the weekend and into the beginning of next week, ensemble \nguidance looks promising in keeping the upper level ridge southeast \nof Colorado, with a few embedded shortwaves in the upper level \nflow. This should keep up relatively cool compared to this past \nweek, with almost daily chances of precipitation.",
